infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title What caps your partition count: the metadata plane
  desc Failover latency and the practical partition ceiling decide how many partitions you can actually run
  compares
    - label ZooKeeper (legacy)
      children
        - label Failover 5–7 seconds
          desc Broker death triggers leader elections that scale with partition count
        - label Ceiling ~1,000 / broker
          desc Past it, producer throughput and p99 latency degrade sharply
        - label Elections scale with N
          desc More partitions means slower controller failover
        - label Throughput collapses by 10K
          desc Confluent tests show it fall from hundreds toward 10K partitions
    - label KRaft (current)
      children
        - label Failover under 1 second
          desc Built-in Raft metadata plane replaces the ZooKeeper dependency
        - label Ceiling 100,000s / cluster
          desc Lifts the practical limit into hundreds of thousands of partitions
        - label Metadata as a log
          desc Controller reads its state from a replicated event log, not ZooKeeper
        - label Headroom to over-provision
          desc Enough room to pick N up front and avoid the one-way-door resize
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
